{"name":"Crocodile Tooth","hex":"#d1ccc2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crocodile","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the predatory amphibious reptiles of the family Crocodylidae;  a crocodilian, any species of the order Crocodilia, which also includes the alligators, caimans and gavials.","A long line or procession of people (especially children) walking together."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To speak another language than Esperanto at an Esperanto-language gathering."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crocodile"},{"term":"tooth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard, calcareous structure present in the mouth of many vertebrate animals, generally used for biting and chewing food.","A sharp projection on the blade of a saw or similar implement."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To provide or furnish with teeth.","To indent; to jag."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tooth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crocodile","blocks":[{"text":"A crocodile or true crocodile is a large, semiaquatic reptile that lives throughout the tropics in Africa, Asia, the Americas and Australia. The term \"crocodile\" is sometimes used more loosely to include all extant members of the order Crocodilia, which includes the alligators and caimans, the gharial and false gharial as well as other extinct taxa. This article uses \"crocodile\" or \"true crocodile\" to refer only to the species taxonomically included in the Crocodylidae family, extant or extinct."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Nile_crocodile_head.jpg/330px-Nile_crocodile_head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crocodile"},{"title":"Tooth","blocks":[{"text":"A tooth is a hard, calcified structure found in the jaws of many vertebrates and used to break down food. Some animals, particularly carnivores and omnivores, also use teeth to help with capturing or wounding prey, tearing food, for defensive purposes, to intimidate other animals often including their own, or to carry prey or their young. The roots of teeth are covered by gums. Teeth are not made of bone, but rather of multiple tissues of varying density and hardness that originate from the outermost embryonic germ layer, the ectoderm."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Close_up_-_chimpanzee_teeth.png/330px-Close_up_-_chimpanzee_teeth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tooth"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Pangil_Crocodile_Park_Davao_City.jpg/330px-Pangil_Crocodile_Park_Davao_City.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Pangil_Crocodile_Park_Davao_City.jpg/330px-Pangil_Crocodile_Park_Davao_City.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APangil_Crocodile_Park_Davao_City.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Crocodylidae_Distribution.png/500px-Crocodylidae_Distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Crocodylidae_Distribution.png/500px-Crocodylidae_Distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrocodylidae_Distribution.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Crocfarm.jpg/500px-Crocfarm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Crocfarm.jpg/1280px-Crocfarm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"http